																		Re: Philosopher&#039;s Blender
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/scorpion451" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1918720); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1918720&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606099748" alt="Scorpion451" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1918720)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/scorpion451" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1918720); return false;" >Scorpion451</a></span> <span class="odate time_1606082843 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">22 Nov 2020 22:07</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4825719">
									

<p>&quot;Magic tool/appliance&quot; and &quot;weird thing that makes more weird things&quot; are both heavily trodden territory on the site, but the concept here might still be viable.</p>
<p>I think you're missing the real potential here, though: A blender that makes super-healthy food isn't really interesting. A blender that blends any two things to produce a new thing resembling both, on the other hand, is <em>very</em> interesting- Blend small electronics. Blend abstract concepts. Blend things with incompatible properties.(say, a lit match and an ice cube) I'd suggest ditching the &quot;decreasing returns&quot; aspect- it doesn't add anything but another reason for the Foundation to put it in a box and ignore it.</p>
<p>On the narrative front- and I apologize for being a bit blunt here in the interest of clarity- what you have right now isn't really a narrative so much as a discovery log, and even within that &quot;it just appeared out of nowhere and then was found by an agent by happenstance&quot; is a site cliche you'll want to avoid. (Compare &quot;It was a dark and stormy night&#8230;&quot;) It's up to you to figure out what sort of story you can tell using with this thing, but on the upside there's a ton of options. I'd suggest brainstorming in the direction of &quot;mad logic&quot;- What intersection of necessity and drunken wishing might spawn this thing? What would drive a mad scientist to usurp the laws of physics to construct it? What might someone with more curiosity than sense do with it? The answer only needs to make sense to the character; walking us through their chain of decisions in a clever way can be a great basis for a story.</p>

							</div>

								
					
