									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,4825748)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-4825748">
																		Re: Philosopher&#039;s Blender
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/seereeni" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7039498); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=7039498&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606099748" alt="Seereeni" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=7039498)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/seereeni" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7039498); return false;" >Seereeni</a></span> <span class="odate time_1606088590 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">22 Nov 2020 23:43</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4825748">
									

<p>Firstly, I would like to thank you for your quick response. I was expecting 1-2 days of waiting until someone review this.</p>
<p>I see, so there would be potential in this SCP idea, potential I was missing. That is&#8230;good to know. However, I wasn't just thinking about food, that was mostly just to show how normal people, in this case kitchen staff, would use it. In the long-term, I was thinking about Foundation personnel, for example, mixing together two different blood types to create a totally new blood type, with it's own pros and cons, pros being immunity to most blood-transmited diseases and cons being that the consumer is constantly thirstly unless drinking blood, human or otherwise.</p>
<p>However, stuff like electronics and incompatible things, that's something I was not really thinking about. Thank you for bringing this possibility to my attention. I will dwell on this idea.</p>
<p>As for the narrative side&#8230;truthfully, I don't really have an idea how this part should work. Yes, i did look up this forum for other ideas posted by others, however thinking that they might be new members and authors like me, I did not take their example for this part.</p>
<p>How should the central narrative look like? What specific&#8230;information should be in it? Like&#8230;</p>
<p><em>The blender is in actuality the experiment of a researcher with a Sc.D in science, who tried to make himself famous. In the end, using the help and resources of a mysterious contributor, he managed to create this blender which, well, mixes together ingredients/materials at an level not yet achieved in modern science. However, said researcher got absorbed, body and mind, into the blender, which now uses his knowledge of science and twisted mind to fine-tune the blending process and create all kinds of unique items. Limited conversation with the researcher can be made using the touchscreen.</em></p>
<p>That was at least the background for this SCP article. Is that a better example of an central narrative? If so, it's slightly hard to explain in this forum with the 400 words limit&#8230;</p>
<p>Nonetheless, I once again thank you for your review.</p>

